{"items":[{"aggregatedOutput":"0dc2762e41dd71817eb2cf7fb236a8970d130a83c67e9db6206ec7e17e125aca  /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-f41391d00c9643a649c2fc911fe4b77a894cf53d022e7882.scope/result.txt\n1956 /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-f41391d00c9643a649c2fc911fe4b77a894cf53d022e7882.scope/result.txt\n","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-f41391d00c9643a649c2fc911fe4b77a894cf53d022e7882.scope/result.txt <<'EOF'\n# How to Automate Blog Creation With AI\n\nAutomate blog creation by giving AI a bounded assignment, supplying approved material, and routing its output to a person for review. Start with an idea and a brief, move through evidence and drafting, and finish with a documented human decision. The workflow can use a shared document and spreadsheet before you connect any automation tools.\n\nFor a small team, one person may fill several roles. Name that person anyway: someone must be accountable for checking evidence and rights, making edits, and deciding what is ready. AI can organize material and flag possible gaps, but its output is something to inspect, not a decision to accept. The steps below are practical editorial recommendations to adapt to your team.\n\n## 1. Select an idea and write a bounded brief\n\nCollect ideas in one intake list. Record the reader's question, where it came from, the intended outcome, and any existing article that might already answer it. A customer question is a useful starting point; it does not by itself establish search demand. Leave missing demand data unknown.\n\nFor topic discovery, ask AI to group supplied questions and suggest possible angles. A person checks the suggestions against the original intake, chooses an idea that fits the audience, and decides whether the team has relevant knowledge and usable evidence. Check whether the answer belongs in an existing article before proposing a new one.\n\nNext, make the assignment narrow enough to review. \u201cExplain our documented process\u201d has a clearer boundary than \u201ccover everything about the industry.\u201d Copy this template for each assignment:\n\n- **Working title and article purpose:**\n- **Intended audience and what they already know:**\n- **Reader outcome and scope:**\n- **Required sections, examples and exclusions:**\n- **Approved source IDs, URLs, versions and permitted use:**\n- **Claims requiring verification and evidence owner:**\n- **Rights requirements and unresolved permissions:**\n- **Tone, format and editorial length target:**\n- **Acceptance criteria, reviewer and decision owner:**\n- **Brief version, date and links to dependent records:**\n\nMake acceptance criteria observable. \u201cExplain the three steps in the approved process note and identify who reviews the result\u201d is easier to assess than \u201cmake it engaging.\u201d State exclusions too, so the writer knows whether pricing, advice outside the team's expertise, or an unverified feature is outside scope.\n\nGoogle's [people-first content guidance](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content) advises creators to focus on helpful, reliable content made for people. Google also publishes guidance on [using generative AI content](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/using-gen-ai-content) and on [generative AI features in Google Search](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/ai-optimization-guide). These describe Google's guidance; they do not promise an outcome for this workflow.\n\n## 2. Establish an approved source set\n\nBefore drafting, have an evidence owner select the materials the article may use. Depending on the assignment, these might include an approved process note, an interview transcript, or a primary document. Record each source's ID, title, URL or storage location, author or owner, publication or update date when available, date checked, and version or preserved snapshot. Note what use is permitted and any attribution or other conditions.\n\nSet a recheck date appropriate to the topic and permission terms. Recheck sooner if a source, the process it describes, or the intended use changes. A source can support a statement without granting permission to reproduce all its material; availability and a citation are not rights clearance.\n\nCreate a claim tracker alongside the source list. Give each factual statement its own ID, identify its location in the draft, and link it to the precise supporting passage in a particular source version. Include reviewer, evidence status, rights status, decision, and recheck date or event. Check a quotation's exact wording and attribution. For each citation, verify that the passage supports the adjacent claim, including its conditions and limits. A link to a reputable home page is not a substitute for the relevant passage.\n\nUse statuses such as **pending**, **verified**, and **blocked**. If support is missing, ambiguous, outdated for the claim, or mismatched, block the claim until a person resolves it or remove it. Treat unresolved permission the same way. AI can assist research by organizing approved excerpts and identifying questions or possible inconsistencies, but a person checks the findings and approves any source before it enters the set.\n\nA simple setup illustrates the handoff. When a draft enters **Needs Review**, an automation can create tracker rows and assign them to the editor. The editor opens the source, checks each passage and rights record, and records the result. If evidence or permission is missing, the row stays blocked with an owner and a recheck date or event; the affected claim and dependent assets remain held for review. Independent work, such as editing an unrelated supported section, can continue while that blocker remains visible. The human decides when it is resolved.\n\n## 3. Draft with AI inside the boundaries\n\nGive the assistant the brief version and approved excerpts. Ask it to distinguish sourced claims from editorial recommendations and invented examples. It can propose an outline, organize notes, draft a section, or identify questions for the evidence owner. Review what it returns against the assignment rather than assuming it followed every instruction.\n\nUse this illustrative prompt as a starting point:\n\n> Using only the approved brief and source excerpts supplied below, draft the article for the specified audience. Stay within the stated scope. Identify each factual claim, quotation and citation with its source ID and precise supporting passage. Mark missing or ambiguous evidence as BLOCKED; do not invent facts, quotations or citations. Separate editorial recommendations and the hypothetical example from sourced claims. Return a draft and a claim-to-source list for human review. Brief: [version and text]. Approved source excerpts: [IDs, versions and excerpts].\n\nThe claim-to-source list is a review aid. Check it against the whole draft: the assistant may omit a claim from its list or point to a passage that does not say what the draft says. If the sources cannot answer a necessary question, return to the evidence owner instead of filling the gap with a plausible sentence.\n\nAdd a relevant perspective that the team can support: explain a decision, document a process, or provide a clearly labeled hypothetical example. Edit for the intended reader, remove repetition, and check that the opening and headings answer the brief. Save the resulting draft as a new version for review.\n\n## 4. Review evidence, rights, and the draft\n\nAn AI review can help assemble possible issues. Supply the same versions used for drafting and ask for locations, not just a general assessment:\n\n> Compare this draft against brief [version] and source set [version]. List every factual claim, quotation and citation with the exact supporting passage, or flag it BLOCKED if support is missing, ambiguous or mismatched. Flag rights questions, scope drift and dependencies affected by changes. Do not assume a citation proves a claim. Return findings with locations and proposed remedies for a human reviewer. Draft: [version]. Source excerpts and rights records: [versions].\n\nA named reviewer checks the draft independently against the brief, actual source passages, and rights records. Read quotations against the original, test links, and check whether each passage supports the full claim. Review headlines and descriptions as well as body text: a shortened headline can overstate a qualified paragraph.\n\nRecord each finding, its location, owner, proposed remedy, and resolution. A correction may mean narrowing the wording, obtaining better evidence or permission, or removing material. Leave an item blocked if it cannot be resolved. The decision owner reviews the findings and records what was approved or blocked and why. An AI review does not replace that responsibility.\n\n## 5. Follow one claim through the tracker\n\nThis example is invented to illustrate the process. It describes no real bakery or result.\n\nA two-person team plans an article about a fictional bakery's bread-making process. The owner defines the scope and supplies material they are authorized to use. The editor opens a tracker with this row:\n\n| Field | Example entry |\n|---|---|\n| Claim | C-01: \u201cThe bakery begins its dough preparation each morning\u201d |\n| Source | S-01, owner-provided process note v1 |\n| Evidence location | Paragraph 2 |\n| Reviewer | Editor |\n| Evidence status | Pending verification |\n| Rights | Pending confirmation |\n| Decision | Blocked |\n\nThe row is a request for checks, not proof that the claim is ready. Once the owner confirms the exact statement and permission, the editor records the evidence and rights decisions before considering release. If either remains unresolved, the claim stays blocked or is removed. The editor also checks any summary or headline that repeats it. Meanwhile, work on an unrelated section can continue without concealing the blocked row.\n\n## 6. Adapt and package the work\n\nAfter article review, prepare channel-specific drafts from the approved article version. Give each asset its own ID, intended destination, owner, and link to the article version. AI can suggest adaptations for different audiences; a person edits and checks each suggestion. Preserve the original meaning and qualifications. Check any new claim, quotation, or material introduced during adaptation against evidence and rights: approval of the article does not automatically approve a changed claim.\n\nCheck the current requirements and rules for each actual destination before considering an asset ready. If the destination or its rules are unknown, mark the channel blocked until someone checks them. For a community post, identify the community and review its current rules. Record media provenance, permission, and any required attribution alongside the asset. An editable original and a rendered version should each have their own version and review status.\n\nAssemble the article, sources and claim tracker, review findings and resolutions, channel assets, and rights records into a package. A linked folder and index can serve this purpose. Mark which items are ready for a decision and which remain blocked. The decision owner decides on the **exact versioned package and its documented blockers**: approve eligible items, request changes, or block the package. Record the person's name, time, package version, what was approved or blocked, and why. Blocked items stay excluded from release until resolved and reviewed. Preparing a package is distinct from permission to publish it.\n\n## 7. Keep versions and decisions connected\n\nPreserve identifiers or immutable snapshots and timestamps for the brief, source set and individual evidence, draft, review findings and resolutions, channel assets, package, and human decision. Link each item to the versions it depends on. A simple chain might look like this:\n\n**Brief v1 \u2192 source set v1 and evidence snapshots \u2192 draft v1 \u2192 review v1 and resolutions \u2192 article v2 \u2192 channel assets v1 \u2192 package v1 \u2192 human decision.**\n\nIf the brief, evidence, or draft changes, identify affected downstream items and reopen their checks. A corrected source might require a claim edit, another article review, and changes to each channel draft that repeats it. Retain prior decisions and their rationale rather than overwriting them, and record the new decision against the new versions.\n\nBefore the decision, use this checklist:\n\n- [ ] The audience, outcome, scope, exclusions, and acceptance criteria are explicit.\n- [ ] Every factual claim, quotation, and citation has verified support in an approved, version-recorded source; unsupported material is removed or blocked.\n- [ ] Rights and permissions for included material are documented; unresolved material is blocked.\n- [ ] A human has reviewed the draft and recorded the decision and unresolved findings.\n- [ ] Version links connect the brief, sources, draft, review, channel assets, package, and decision.\n- [ ] Hypothetical material is labeled, and AI review is treated as assistance rather than verification.\n\nPick one article and run it through this sequence. Keep the brief, tracker, and decision record together, including anything that stayed blocked. Use what the team learns to adjust the next brief and its handoffs.\nEOF\nsha256sum /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-f41391d00c9643a649c2fc911fe4b77a894cf53d022e7882.scope/result.txt\nwc -w /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workflows/data/marketing-tools38/local-outputs/agent-workflows-f41391d00c9643a649c2fc911fe4b77a894cf53d022e7882.scope/result.txt\"","commandActions":[{"command":"cat > /var/home/user/Documents/agent-workf